00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:25.160
already.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:30.880
Next, we're going to talk a
little bit about BeyondCorp.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.870
Now, starting in 2011, Google
introduced a new approach

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:38.050
to how to do access management,
which was called BeyondCorp

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:42.420
Security Model.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.850
It's a zero-trust security
model, and what that means is,

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.447
you don't rely on the
permissions or specific network

00:03:47.447 --> 00:03:49.030
configurations or
making sure somebody

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.600
has to reside on your
corporate network

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.130
in order to gain access.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:56.670
But instead, it verifies
properties of the requester,

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.180
making sure that this requester
has access and permissions

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.900
to the specific resources
that they're gaining access.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.470
So it shifts the focus from
using network perimeter

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:12.250
to individual users and devices
and using those in order

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.410
to decide whether or not
somebody should gain access.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:18.100
And this allows employees to
work seamlessly, regardless

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.008
of where they're located.

00:04:20.008 --> 00:04:21.800
So we're going to spend
a couple of minutes

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.020
and look a little bit
closer into BeyondCorp

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:27.090
and how it can be used
in a hybrid environment.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.400
Before we get there though,
just going a little deeper

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.310
into BeyondCorp, it has--

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:34.560
For BeyondCorp, there's
several different components

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.600
that are needed in order to be
able to make up the solution.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.920
One is a notion of
strong user identity,

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.560
and while it doesn't
require you to use

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.420
phishing-resistant
technology, it definitely

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:48.592
works very well with that.

00:04:48.592 --> 00:04:50.300
So you can use notions
like security keys

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.675
in order to make sure that
the requester that's coming in

00:04:52.675 --> 00:04:55.800
is actually accessing the
location that they need.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.630
It also leverages
context information

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.760
about their request,
so things like

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.160
where the request
is originating from,

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.530
and in addition, looks at
things like device posture

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:07.290
to see what type of device is
this request actually coming

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:08.610
from, as well.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:10.260
Using those two
types of information,

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.900
it pulls it into a
centralized policy

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:16.110
engine that allows both
IT teams to go define

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.960
these types of policies
that says only devices that

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:20.820
have the following
security postures

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.620
and these specific
classes of users

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:25.880
can get access to this
resource, and then uses

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.950
a centralized enforcement
point in order

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:30.380
to be able to ensure
that this type of access

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:32.930
is consistently administered
regardless of where

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:36.205
a person is coming in from.

00:05:36.205 --> 00:05:40.690
So Google Cloud provides a
solution for the BeyondCorp

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:43.300
product, and it's comprised
of several different products

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.720
inside of Google, one of
which is Cloud Identity that

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.550
allows you to take
care of the identity

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:50.710
as well as the context
information in terms

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:54.070
of what devices this request
is actually coming in from.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:56.800
There's another component
around a rules engine,

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.320
and that's done through
Access Context Manager that

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:02.070
allows you to go in, look
at information around kind

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:06.100
of origination of the
request, device posture,

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:07.720
in order to make
that authentication

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.450
and authorization decision.

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:13.320
We also use VPC security
control service controls

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:15.930
that improve your ability
to mitigate the risk of data

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.510
exfiltration from Google
managed services things

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:20.940
like Cloud Storage, BigQuery.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:23.760
You can actually do a boundary
around all these services

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.890
to ensure that all your data
retains within those walls.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:31.580
It also uses IAM, which is
Identity and Access Management,

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:36.340
that allows you to specify
granular controls on resources

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:38.470
to decide whether or not
a specific user should

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:41.150
have access to that resource.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:44.030
And the final piece
is really Cloud IAP.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:47.510
Now Cloud IAP, or
Identity-Aware Proxy,

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:52.040
is a GCP service that provides
off-the-shelf authentication

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.280
and authorization
for applications

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.460
that are deployed on GCP.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:01.280
It is also the starting
point for many customers

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:05.540
to retire their legacy
VPNs and use Google's

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:09.200
context-aware access story.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.900
But while this works
great for scenarios

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.210
that exist on Google
Fabric, the reality

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:15.770
is many customers
have applications

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:18.170
that actually run either
in an on-prem environment

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:19.850
or in another cloud.

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:22.490
So IAP also now
supports the ability

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:26.067
to protect your workloads no
matter where they're located.

00:07:26.067 --> 00:07:28.400
Numerous customers are now
routing traffic that actually

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:32.180
goes through Google's front
ends, leverages IAP, and then

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:35.190
forwards the encrypted traffic
back to their applications,

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:37.220
no matter where
that application is,

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:40.160
and the best part is, there
are no changes required

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:41.300
to the application.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.280
It all happens right
in front of it,

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:45.060
adding a layer of protection.

00:07:48.122 --> 00:07:49.580
So we're going to
walk through this

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:52.850
in a little bit more detail.

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:54.590
When a user actually
connects in,

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:56.570
one of the things
that happens is,

00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:58.670
the device posture
and device information

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:01.160
is captured and
continuously updated in one

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:03.310
of the databases.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:05.370
When a request comes
into the system,

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:08.120
GCLB provides network
layer protection.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:12.010
So it can do things like
protect against DDoS attacks,

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:15.400
provides TLS termination,
and also manages certificates

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:19.450
and allows you to configure
things like IP block lists.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:26.595
The identity-aware proxy users
the user identity as well as

00:08:26.595 --> 00:08:27.970
device information
in order to be

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:31.400
able to make access decisions.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:35.492
In this case, you'll see
the IAP on-prem connector,

00:08:35.492 --> 00:08:37.159
which is the component
that allows you--

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:39.950
it's an open source tool
that allows you to connect

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:43.505
to a on-prem back end resource.

00:08:43.505 --> 00:08:45.130
One of the things
that needs to be done

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:47.350
is for that on-prem
application, you

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:49.000
need to configure
the network policy

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.880
so that only requests that
originate through the IAP front

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.970
end system will be able to get
access in order to truly deploy

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:56.530
this model end-to-end.

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:58.540
Once that's configured,
the BeyondCorp model

00:08:58.540 --> 00:09:00.400
works completely
for these scenarios.

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:05.600
So that was the first section
talking a little bit about how

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.760
you can use GCP in
a hybrid environment

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:09.390
where your users are
either configured

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:11.750
in an external
identity source on prem

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:14.300
or coming from a
external IDaaS system,

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.340
talked a little bit
about BeyondCorp.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:19.722
Now we're going to
switch into Kubernetes,

00:09:19.722 --> 00:09:21.680
and then we'll talk about
how you can configure

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:25.340
authentication and authorization
into Kubernetes clusters using

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.365
your existing enterprise
infrastructure.

00:09:27.365 --> 00:09:29.448
For that, I'll switch over
to my colleague, Breno.

00:09:29.448 --> 00:09:30.364
BRENO DE MEDEIROS:
Thank you, Naveen.

00:09:30.364 --> 00:09:31.280
NAVEEN CHAND: Thanks.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:33.420
BRENO DE MEDEIROS:
So in this section,

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:36.020
we'll be looking into
how to authenticate

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:40.070
into the Kubernetes control
plane for cluster management

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:41.460
activities.

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:45.140
So we'll focus on the
usage of the Kubernetes API

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:46.730
through this
standard command line

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:51.800
interface, or CLI for short,
which is called kubectl.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.830
And the same principles
apply to other means

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:59.330
of interacting with the
control plane in Kubernetes.

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:01.820
For instance, if you use any
of the controller devices.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:08.130
So in this scenario that we
are going to look into here,

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:10.860
we have Jeanine is
a cloud engineer,

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:13.860
and she works for a
company called Zotkix.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:18.486
She's just finished creating
their first Kubernetes cluster.

00:10:18.486 --> 00:10:22.700
As Jeanine went through the
steps to create the cluster,

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:25.150
those steps give
her admin privilege,

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:28.260
which is natural because she's
just created the cluster.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:33.180
But it's based on secrets that
are stored in a kubeconfig file

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:34.020
typically.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:36.260
That's the outcome of
creating a Kubernetes cluster

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:40.380
through most of the procedures.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:42.890
But Zotkix's security
policies outline

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:48.610
a set of requirements for
access to production assets.

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:51.300
And these requirements
are not met

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:54.180
by secrets is stored
in configuration

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:58.840
files in administrators'
laptops and other devices.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:01.950
So we need to find a
different solution that

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:04.110
is enterprise compatible
and compatible

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:08.430
with security posture of
most enterprise environments.

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:11.910
So indeed, in most
enterprise environments,

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:13.860
integrating the
SSO infrastructure

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:18.490
is required to manage
the production workloads.

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:22.020
So this involves two
steps, effectively.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:24.480
One is to connect
the authentication

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:27.670
and the credential
Management to the SSO system,

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:30.210
and the other is to
configure permissions

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:33.610
based on the centrally
managed groups.

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:36.550
So we will look into each
of these steps in detail.

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:42.150
Well, first what is available
in Kubernetes today?

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:45.180
So Kubernetes supports several
mechanisms to authenticate

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:49.920
into a cluster, but for
integration with SSO systems,

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:53.310
Kubernetes supports in
particular OpenIDConnect, which

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:59.420
also known as OIDC, and Active
Directory Federation Services,

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:05.750
or ADFS for short, is a
commonly deployed enterprise

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:10.110
system, part of the Active
Directory set of services.

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:13.400
And it implements an
OpenIDConnect provider,

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:15.530
identity provider, or IDP.

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:17.840
So this provides
the means for us

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:21.140
to enable enterprise single
sign on into a cluster.

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:26.870
I'm assuming in this talk
that we're using ADFS.

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:29.640
We're going to go into some
configuration details here,

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:33.260
but many other enterprises
solutions, such as Google Cloud

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:36.180
Identity, the Google Cloud
Identity platform, Azure

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:39.680
AD, Okta, to all support OIDC.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:44.150
So now we're going
to show a demo

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:49.360
on how to register
OIDC client for ADFS.

