gets generated is random.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:42.440
So it just makes painful
to copy and paste,

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:48.060
but I'll copy it in here and
remember it as my client ID.

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:51.240
Next I need to have a
register redirect URL.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:52.980
I should first
choose the port that

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:56.170
is widely available in my
enterprise environment.

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:59.190
And then I can register our URL
that the points to that port.

00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:02.100
The reason is that we
need a place to redirect

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:05.580
the token after
the authentication

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:08.970
to the user's workstation.

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:10.860
And here we call also
the client secret

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:14.730
that gets autogenerated
when we enable secret base

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:17.510
authentication.

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:22.000
So I finished creating an
open-ended client in ADFS,

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:27.640
but now I need to configure
a token group's rule, which

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:30.370
allow me to share a group's
information from Active

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:34.000
Directory with the
Kubernetes cluster

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:37.130
so we can do role
based access control.

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:47.390
So I have to give it some
descriptive information

00:14:47.390 --> 00:14:49.110
so I can remember it.

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:52.520
But the important
thing here is also

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:58.580
that I need to authorize
later to grant us essentially

00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:03.410
permissions for ADFS to
share that information

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:05.750
to this specific
Kubernetes client

00:15:05.750 --> 00:15:09.140
that I registered
in the first step.

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:12.880
So I created the rule.

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:16.240
Now I'm actually saying what
I'm going to populate it with,

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:19.890
what is going to be
propagated by that rule.

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:27.990
And we'll configure this
is to use LDAP groups.

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:29.980
It's called the token groups.

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:32.940
So I'm using token groups
qualified by long domain

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.550
name, which gives them
greater security because you

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:38.310
can't have collisions that way.

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:43.170
And I'm naming the
outputting token as groups.

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:46.600
That's going to be the
claim name in the token,

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:48.410
and I'm remembering
all of this information

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:51.260
because I'm going to have
to configure my client,

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:54.710
as well as the API server
to be able to understand

00:15:54.710 --> 00:15:57.170
how to process the
token information.

00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:59.240
So finally, now
that I've finished

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:02.390
creating the configuration
of the client

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:06.500
and of how to share
the group's claims,

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:10.700
I need to grant
authorization to the client

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:14.550
that I created to receive
group's information.

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:16.190
So that's the last
command that I'm

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:19.670
going to run in the Windows
PowerShell as an administrator.

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:26.440
And we are done with
this first step.

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:32.580
And if you have a different
IDP, Okta or something, Cloud

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:34.905
Identity Platform,
Google Cloud Identity,

00:16:34.905 --> 00:16:36.780
you're going to have a
different set of steps

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:38.238
to configure the
identity provider,

00:16:38.238 --> 00:16:41.610
but most of the subsequent
steps in this conversation

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:45.220
here will be generic
for any provider.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:48.540
So we have now some handy notes
that we took during our client

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:54.000
registration with the parameters
for OpenIDConnect client

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:55.770
that we registered.

00:16:55.770 --> 00:17:00.870
But the kubectl CLI, the
command line interface,

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:05.369
also needs those parameters
to integrate with the IDP.

00:17:05.369 --> 00:17:09.480
But in addition to the
information about the client,

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:11.700
basically, the OIDC
client, kubectl

00:17:11.700 --> 00:17:14.010
also needs some information
about the provider

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:17.470
itself, such as where
is the provider?

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:21.599
So we will show how to get
that information from ADFS.

00:17:21.599 --> 00:17:24.550
OK so here, there are
two pieces of information

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:26.849
that I need about
the provider itself

00:17:26.849 --> 00:17:28.960
for my kubectl configuration.

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:31.430
One is where's the provider?

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:34.530
So I'm going to start trying--

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:39.100
I'm here at the ADFS server,
and it has the host information,

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:40.390
has the domain information.

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:44.890
So that gives me the
domain part of this URL.

00:17:44.890 --> 00:17:49.150
And next I'm going to look
into the endpoint configuration

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:52.390
for ADFS because ADFS
has many endpoints that

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:55.400
serve different properties.

00:17:55.400 --> 00:17:58.250
I look at the
OpenIDConnect endpoints,

00:17:58.250 --> 00:18:00.200
and what is important
is there is something

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:02.100
that starts to dot well-known.

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:06.690
The part of the path before that
is where the ADFS instance is.

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:09.775
So I got that section of
the path is very typical.

00:18:09.775 --> 00:18:10.400
It's just ADFS.

00:18:13.790 --> 00:18:16.740
And now I want to
get the certificate.

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:19.400
So I'm looking into
the certificate

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:22.970
that the ADFS server uses
because the Kubernetes

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:24.980
cluster is not
part of my domain,

00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:27.170
and it doesn't know the
intermediate certificates

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:30.980
that I have deployed in
my services, such as ADFS.

00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:34.500
So I'm going through
the certificate two,

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:38.840
and I am going to export the
intermediate certificate that

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:42.170
is used by the ADFS server.

00:18:42.170 --> 00:18:46.040
So I've just used
the Export Wizard,

00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:50.598
and I had chosen a file name.

00:18:50.598 --> 00:18:51.640
So I'm going to use that.

00:18:56.170 --> 00:18:59.730
And we are finished with of
the parameters from the issuer.

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:03.190
OK, so now we have the
OIDC client parameters,

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:05.162
and we have the
issuer parameters.

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:10.840
Now we go to the API server.

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:14.010
We need to configure the
API server to actually

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:19.460
accept sign ins from this
OIDC client that we created.

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:23.870
So this involves
restarting the API server

00:19:23.870 --> 00:19:28.350
with a set of flags that
enable OIDC authentication.

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:30.800
So this I had from
my handy notes

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:34.820
that I took while I did
the previous two demos,

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:37.760
and this is showing how
I can configure the flags

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:39.000
with those parameters.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.960
And if I restart the API
server with those parameters,

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:47.215
it will be able to accept
OIDC authentication.

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:54.210
Now we are finally ready to
prepare kubectl to authenticate

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:55.370
to the cluster.

00:19:55.370 --> 00:19:59.360
And kubectl remember, it needed
the OIDC client parameters

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:01.460
that we registered with ADFS.

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:03.740
It needs some server
parameters that we

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:07.760
got from ADFS
configuration itself,

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:10.590
but it also needs some
parameters from the cluster

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:11.900
so it knows how to talk to it.

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:12.400
OK?

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:13.900
So that's the last
set of parameters

00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:16.850
that we need to finish this.

00:20:16.850 --> 00:20:23.030
So recall that right after
Jeanine created the cluster,

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:25.940
she had the usable configuration
that gave her admin

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:28.350
privileges in the cluster.

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:30.590
So that from that
configuration actually

00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:32.810
has all the cluster
parameters that we need.

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:35.810
So we are going to go look
into that file, the kubeconfig

00:20:35.810 --> 00:20:39.650
file that was initially created
to get those parameters so

00:20:39.650 --> 00:20:44.600
that we can package all of this
in a single file that will make

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:47.930
it much easier for all the
other administrators, as well

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:50.810
as the other users,
developers in the cluster,

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:53.950
to be able to authenticate.

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:57.930
So let's see how we can
package all this metadata.

00:20:57.930 --> 00:21:04.820
So we have created a plug-in
to facilitate users to sign in.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:08.510
Today, to get access to it, you
needed to sign in for the GKE

00:21:08.510 --> 00:21:10.650
On-Prem .

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:11.480
offering.

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:13.880
But if you-- in
the future, we plan

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:15.840
to make it more
widely available.

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:20.640
So once you download this
plugin, you make it executable,

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:26.360
and if you move it to a
path, location in your path,

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:28.890
it will be available
for kubectl.

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:32.090
So now I have collected in
this file all the parameters

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:35.210
from the three steps
that I went before,

00:21:35.210 --> 00:21:38.150
and I configured them as
a single command which

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:39.630
is kubectl.

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:43.790
OIDC is the name of our plugin
that was just installed,

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:47.300
and client config is the
name of the parameter.

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:51.950
Once we run, we need a
couple of the parameters

00:21:51.950 --> 00:21:54.720
that I explained from
the cluster itself.

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:57.800
So now I open that
early kubeconfig file,

00:21:57.800 --> 00:22:00.430
and I found the server name.

00:22:00.430 --> 00:22:01.130
OK?

00:22:01.130 --> 00:22:04.770
And I'm back into that file.

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:07.570
The server, your location,
the server name, and I

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:12.520
need the server
certificate authority.

00:22:12.520 --> 00:22:15.270
So I gave it a
name, and I'm going

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:18.940
to export from there the--

00:22:18.940 --> 00:22:20.860
this was the certificate
that was embedded

00:22:20.860 --> 00:22:24.700
in the original
kubectl config file,

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:27.820
and I'm going to
basically base64 decode it

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:32.080
into a certificate file.

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:34.440
So now I have everything ready.

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:37.740
So you have a properly
formatted certificate stored.

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:39.360
I have all the parameters.

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:42.230
I can run that command.

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:45.080
And I have a file.

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:49.520
And so that is completely
configured with client

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:54.920
information, OIDC issuer
information, and cluster

00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:58.230
information that kubectl needs
to be able to authenticate.

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:00.440
So this was a lot of steps.

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:03.620
You definitely don't want
more than one administrator

00:23:03.620 --> 00:23:05.040
to have to go through this.

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:06.920
So that's why we
created this plugin.

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:11.580
This plugin wraps all the
parameters in one easy file.

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:13.190
And now you can
distribute this file

00:23:13.190 --> 00:23:16.040
to all the users in
your organization.

00:23:16.040 --> 00:23:17.060
OK?

00:23:17.060 --> 00:23:21.650
So let's see how we can
actually use this file

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:27.410
to enable all users in the
organization to easily sign in

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:29.120
with OpenIDConnect.

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:33.300
OK.

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:37.020
And so we are again using
the same plugin, but now

00:23:37.020 --> 00:23:38.130
the login command.

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:40.440
I chose a username for myself.

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:44.200
I passed the config file that
the administrator sent to me.

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:46.710
And I chose a kubeconfig
name where I start

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:48.900
my kubeconfig information.

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:54.800
So I'm signing in using
the ADFS sign-in page.

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:55.460
That's it.

00:23:55.460 --> 00:23:58.170
That's a user experience,
and they are ready to go.

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:04.260
So now you have everything
starting this kubeconfig file.

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:06.500
I'm going to set up as
my environment variable,

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:10.400
so I can now just start
performing operations

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:12.920
of the cluster, and
I'm authenticated

00:24:12.920 --> 00:24:16.300
using single sign-on
and leveraging

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:17.660
Kubernetes authorization.

00:24:17.660 --> 00:24:21.560
So a lot of steps for the
first administrator, extremely

00:24:21.560 --> 00:24:23.900
easy for other administrator,
other users later once

00:24:23.900 --> 00:24:24.900
that file is packed.

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:26.990
You just have to run.

00:24:26.990 --> 00:24:27.920
That's all.

00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:31.400
OK.

00:24:31.400 --> 00:24:36.393
Let's look now a little more
into how Kubernetes RBAC works.

00:24:36.393 --> 00:24:38.310
Because we've figured
out how to authenticate,

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:41.310
now how do we grant
privileges to the cluster?

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:46.350
So Zotkix, again, bringing
back to their perspective,

00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:49.530
wants to segment privileges
within the cluster.

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:52.130
So one situation
could be that they

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:55.160
require a strict separation
between production

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:57.660
and development workloads.

00:24:57.660 --> 00:25:01.010
So Kubernetes allows you to
compartmentalize permissions

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:03.710
in this way through namespaces.

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:08.240
And administrators can delegate
permissions to a namespace.

00:25:08.240 --> 00:25:11.680
Permissions can be
attached to a role,

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:17.680
and there are two types of
roles in Kubernetes RBAC.

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:20.530
One is a role with
cluster-wide scope

00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:22.390
that's called a cluster role.

00:25:22.390 --> 00:25:25.810
And you want a role that
has namespace-wide scope,

00:25:25.810 --> 00:25:27.160
and that's just called role.

00:25:29.680 --> 00:25:33.740
Roles can be granted to users
and groups using bindings.

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:34.600
OK?

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:38.710
So a cluster role binding
binds is a cluster role

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:41.800
to a user or a group,
and the binding

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:45.150
has a scope across
a cluster-wide.

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:47.650
A role binding,
on the other hand,

00:25:47.650 --> 00:25:53.830
can bind either a cluster role
or a role to a user or a group,

00:25:53.830 --> 00:25:58.990
but the binding has
namespace scope.

00:25:58.990 --> 00:26:02.620
In the next demo, we'll show
how a Zotkix administrator

00:26:02.620 --> 00:26:06.595
can use bindings to grant
roles to users and groups.

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:14.170
So I will now show demo of how
to set up the Kubernetes RBAC.

00:26:16.930 --> 00:26:18.130
I can see my AD.

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:20.000
I have two users.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:24.790
One is Breno, and if you
look at the properties,

00:26:24.790 --> 00:26:28.600
he is part of a group
called cluster admin.

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:30.440
OK?

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:35.450
But we also have another
user called unprivileged,

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:38.210
and as we would
expect, he is not

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:41.460
a member of any group
except domain users.

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:43.710
OK?

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:54.870
So I am logged in to kubectl
as myself, using the plugin as

00:26:54.870 --> 00:26:59.180
before, and I'm going to get
cluster roles for this cluster.

00:26:59.180 --> 00:27:02.710
There's a bunch
of them, and I see

00:27:02.710 --> 00:27:05.540
one called cluster admin
that looks suggestive.

00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:10.230
So let's look, find more
about that cluster role.

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:12.780
So I am going to
inspect the cluster

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:20.260
role called cluster admin, and
I see that it is a cluster role.

00:27:20.260 --> 00:27:22.720
The name is cluster admin.

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:25.250
And it is granted a
lot of privileges.

00:27:25.250 --> 00:27:25.750
OK?

00:27:25.750 --> 00:27:30.220
It can do every operation on
every resource on every API

00:27:30.220 --> 00:27:32.910
group in the Kubernetes API.

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:33.970
Great.

00:27:33.970 --> 00:27:38.780
Who can actually use that,
exercise that cluster role?

00:27:38.780 --> 00:27:42.070
Well, I see also a role
binding called cluster admin.

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:44.050
Maybe that's the
one I'm looking for.

00:27:44.050 --> 00:27:48.400
Let's look at this cluster
admin role binding.

00:27:48.400 --> 00:27:49.330
OK.

00:27:49.330 --> 00:27:51.460
It is a cluster role binding.

00:27:51.460 --> 00:27:55.570
The name is cluster
admin, and it

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:59.110
refers to the cluster
role, cluster admin

00:27:59.110 --> 00:28:00.330
that I had before.

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:01.990
So it looks all promising.

00:28:01.990 --> 00:28:06.780
But the subject is
a system object.

00:28:06.780 --> 00:28:09.810
That doesn't sound like
the right cluster role.

00:28:09.810 --> 00:28:14.680
I would expect users, groups
coming from AD in that setting.

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:17.430
So I'm going back to my
list of role bindings,

00:28:17.430 --> 00:28:19.950
and I found something else
called Cluster Manager.

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:21.720
Is that the right one?

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:23.685
Let's look at what the
Cluster Manager has.

00:28:26.300 --> 00:28:29.400
It looks very similar,
but the main difference

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:34.890
is that the subject is the group
called cluster admin coming

00:28:34.890 --> 00:28:37.230
from the SSO environment.

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:41.800
So that's indeed
the binding that

00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:46.238
is granting me privileges to
perform all these operations.

00:28:46.238 --> 00:28:48.030
OK?

00:28:48.030 --> 00:28:51.860
Now I'm going to try and do the
same as the unprivileged user,

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:53.970
OK?

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:57.660
And if I remand this operation,
I can see that instead

00:28:57.660 --> 00:29:00.600
of looking at the cluster
roles, I'm getting an error,

00:29:00.600 --> 00:29:05.850
that they user with a
long name qualified there,

00:29:05.850 --> 00:29:10.390
cannot list resources cluster
roles because they're not

00:29:10.390 --> 00:29:13.450
a part of the cluster
admin group, and therefore,

00:29:13.450 --> 00:29:16.360
they are not bound to
the Cluster Manager role

00:29:16.360 --> 00:29:18.580
in the cluster.

00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:22.960
We talked quite a bit
about user authentication.

00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:27.050
I will talk about
workload identity now.

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:32.720
So workload identity
in Kubernetes,

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:35.550
there is a pre built-in
system for workload identities

00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:40.250
in Kubernetes when we
use the Kubernetes API.

00:29:40.250 --> 00:29:45.350
This form of Kubernetes identity
is called a service account,

00:29:45.350 --> 00:29:48.920
and it's created in a namespace.

00:29:48.920 --> 00:29:52.400
So Kubernetes service accounts
may be granted permissions

00:29:52.400 --> 00:29:57.020
either via cluster role bindings
or role bindings, just as users

00:29:57.020 --> 00:29:59.263
and groups.

00:29:59.263 --> 00:30:02.270
So, in this example
I'm showing here

00:30:02.270 --> 00:30:13.020
how Jenkins CI/CD workload can
be granted permission to modify

00:30:13.020 --> 00:30:16.320
all pods in a cluster.

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:18.910
It also shows how the
same permission can be--

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:20.980
so, if you look here--

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:23.170
can I see the--

00:30:23.170 --> 00:30:25.520
I don't see my--

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:27.520
oh.

00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:28.230
Oh well, sorry.

00:30:28.230 --> 00:30:32.640
I don't see the pointer, so I'm
just going to point directly.

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:35.430
You have a cluster
row called pod leader

00:30:35.430 --> 00:30:38.580
which is given permissions to
modify pods in the cluster.

00:30:41.790 --> 00:30:46.800
We have a cluster of binding
that binds this privilege

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:49.350
to a service account
called CI/CD,

00:30:49.350 --> 00:30:52.870
which is your continuous
deployment tool.

00:30:52.870 --> 00:30:56.970
And so, naturally, your
continuous deployment

00:30:56.970 --> 00:31:00.900
needs to update
jobs in the cluster.

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:07.000
And so, the granting seal refers
to the cluster HomePod updater.

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:09.720
So, this allows
a workload that's

00:31:09.720 --> 00:31:13.710
running the cluster with
this service identity which

00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:17.565
runs in the production namespace
and with name CI/CD to modify

00:31:17.565 --> 00:31:19.680
all pods in the cluster.

00:31:19.680 --> 00:31:24.450
There is a separate
row binding here

00:31:24.450 --> 00:31:29.850
that gives to the dev team
the ability to exercise also

00:31:29.850 --> 00:31:38.490
the pod updater role, but only
in the namespace development.

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:41.640
And so, if you have
a production job,

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:43.200
they will not be
able to modify it.

00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:55.360
We now have support for
workload identity in GKE

00:31:55.360 --> 00:32:00.460
to directly exercise
privileges in GCP IAM.

00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:06.740
So, that makes it much easier
to secure access to your GCP

00:32:06.740 --> 00:32:10.510
resources, because you no longer
need to download and manage

00:32:10.510 --> 00:32:13.600
GCP service accounts in GKE.

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:18.100
Instead, with workload
identity, the provision

00:32:18.100 --> 00:32:23.170
of credentials for GKE
services can be fully managed.

00:32:23.170 --> 00:32:27.440
So, if you look at
the parallel here--

00:32:27.440 --> 00:32:29.220
in Kubernetes RBAC.

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:32.310
If you wanted to create,
to grant privileges,

00:32:32.310 --> 00:32:35.610
you create the cluster role
and you create cluster role

00:32:35.610 --> 00:32:40.530
bindings where you grant
those rows privileges

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:43.600
to specific users or
groups or service accounts.

00:32:43.600 --> 00:32:51.870
In GCP IAM, RBAC, you
have a service account--

00:32:51.870 --> 00:32:56.170
GCP service account--
and what you do

00:32:56.170 --> 00:33:01.040
is you grant this
service account

00:33:01.040 --> 00:33:05.310
access to some properties
in your project.

00:33:05.310 --> 00:33:09.320
So, here, I'm granting
this GCP service account

00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:12.650
an editor role in the project.

00:33:12.650 --> 00:33:18.510
Then, you can exercise that
role from a Kubernetes workload

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:24.270
by adding a policy
binding that mentions

00:33:24.270 --> 00:33:28.540
a workload identity in GKE.

00:33:28.540 --> 00:33:34.390
So, that's what this
expression is describing here.

00:33:34.390 --> 00:33:37.630
If you recall from the
example that we gave,

00:33:37.630 --> 00:33:40.090
the namespace production
and the service

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:44.030
account CI/CD represented my
continuous deployment tool.

00:33:44.030 --> 00:33:46.450
So, with this
configuration, you don't

00:33:46.450 --> 00:33:51.310
need to create this downloader
key into your GKE deployed to.

00:33:51.310 --> 00:33:54.830
It can directly access GCP.

00:33:59.090 --> 00:34:03.227
OK, and this-- courtesy to
our friends in the GKE team--

00:34:03.227 --> 00:34:04.935
I have a demonstration
of how this works.

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:10.630
First we are creating
a works cluster.

00:34:10.630 --> 00:34:12.850
There is a flag
called identity space.

00:34:12.850 --> 00:34:16.060
That tells us that this should
enable the workload identity

00:34:16.060 --> 00:34:20.080
feature in the cluster.

00:34:20.080 --> 00:34:21.960
So, we're looking at
the configuration here.

00:34:21.960 --> 00:34:23.699
I have an app called GCS server.

00:34:28.250 --> 00:34:33.929
And, it has an annotation
which refers to a GCP service

00:34:33.929 --> 00:34:35.620
account.

00:34:35.620 --> 00:34:43.000
So, that tells our
metadata service w

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:47.739
to allow the workload identity
to get tokens to talk to GCP.

00:34:47.739 --> 00:34:50.500
And this all happens
transparently with no changes

00:34:50.500 --> 00:34:54.100
to the application or the
provision of credentials

00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:59.880
is using the
infrastructure of GKE.

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:05.070
So, just for reference, we
have the namespace and service

00:35:05.070 --> 00:35:08.640
account and workload identity
service account there.

00:35:08.640 --> 00:35:12.000
We are creating that GCP
account we referred to

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:12.930
in the annotation.

00:35:18.780 --> 00:35:20.850
And we are seeing
that the application

00:35:20.850 --> 00:35:23.910
that we first constructed
is now running as deployed.

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:31.460
We want to grant the
binding that I've

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:35.600
talked about where we give
the Kubernetes service

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:39.890
account the ability to
impersonate the GCP service

00:35:39.890 --> 00:35:43.370
account to exercise that role.

00:35:43.370 --> 00:35:46.750
Whoops, something
is not working.

00:35:46.750 --> 00:35:50.030
What could possibly go wrong?

00:35:50.030 --> 00:35:53.930
We forgot to grant, actually,
privileges to our GCP service

00:35:53.930 --> 00:35:56.480
account that we just created.

00:35:56.480 --> 00:35:59.300
So, it is successful
impersonating the GCP service

00:35:59.300 --> 00:36:02.850
account, but this service
account has no privileges.

00:36:02.850 --> 00:36:03.890
So, we can fix that.

00:36:06.670 --> 00:36:08.370
In a minute, you'll
see how to fix that.

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:16.140
The error is access denied.

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:19.900
So, you see it mentioned
the GCP service account.

00:36:19.900 --> 00:36:22.260
So the error was--

00:36:22.260 --> 00:36:24.900
so now we add the permission--

00:36:24.900 --> 00:36:28.980
see that's Echo-- and we gave
the privileges to our GCP

00:36:28.980 --> 00:36:32.580
service account permission,
and now the job is

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:36.480
able to perform its operations,
correctly fetch the results,

00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:37.260
and display them.

00:36:40.060 --> 00:36:46.030
And this is showing that this
is a multi-cluster feature.

00:36:46.030 --> 00:36:50.260
You can enable the
same identity--

00:36:50.260 --> 00:36:54.970
the same role be used
by multiple clusters.

00:36:54.970 --> 00:36:59.140
And so, we are deploying
this server in a different--

00:36:59.140 --> 00:37:03.430
you can see east can
access and so can west.

00:37:03.430 --> 00:37:07.255
All right, they both can
access their GCS resource.

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:11.980
However, if you
delete that echo--

00:37:11.980 --> 00:37:14.130
the echo on the
service account--

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:16.730
you can see that the
application no longer

00:37:16.730 --> 00:37:19.500
can access the same resource.

00:37:19.500 --> 00:37:21.650
And you'll go back
to that scenario

00:37:21.650 --> 00:37:25.690
where you don't have privilege.

00:37:25.690 --> 00:37:30.440
OK, so that finishes our
demonstration of workload

00:37:30.440 --> 00:37:31.760
identity and Kubernetes.

00:37:35.470 --> 00:37:38.230
And now I'm going to
give you an overview

00:37:38.230 --> 00:37:42.890
of identity authentication
in a hybrid environment.

00:37:42.890 --> 00:37:49.380
So, EU Istio an open
source mesh that

00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:52.940
lays transparently on the
existing and distributed

00:37:52.940 --> 00:37:54.480
services.

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:57.170
And it's also
platform, including

00:37:57.170 --> 00:38:00.680
APIs that integrate with any
logging, telemetry, or policy

00:38:00.680 --> 00:38:01.910
system.

00:38:01.910 --> 00:38:06.740
So, why should we consider using
Istio in my hybrid environment?

00:38:06.740 --> 00:38:09.080
So, Istio makes
it easy to create

00:38:09.080 --> 00:38:12.710
a network of deployed
services with load balancing,

00:38:12.710 --> 00:38:15.760
service to service
authentication, monitoring,

00:38:15.760 --> 00:38:21.360
and more with few or no code
changes in service code.

00:38:21.360 --> 00:38:24.720
And it also can be
used to deliver secure,

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:27.330
service to service
communication in a cluster

00:38:27.330 --> 00:38:30.225
with a strong identity-based
authentication

00:38:30.225 --> 00:38:34.880
and authorization
based on mutual TLS.

00:38:34.880 --> 00:38:38.880
And Google is a major
contributor of today's Istio

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:40.870
development and architecture.

00:38:40.870 --> 00:38:42.952
So, it means that
by using Istio you

00:38:42.952 --> 00:38:46.320
take advantage of some of the
best practices and strategies

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:48.698
from micro-service
mesh management.

00:38:51.770 --> 00:38:55.520
So, to begin, you add
Istio support to services

00:38:55.520 --> 00:39:01.310
by deploying a sidecar proxy
throughout your environment.

00:39:01.310 --> 00:39:04.100
And this sidecar
proxy intercepts

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:09.360
all network communication
between the microservices.

00:39:09.360 --> 00:39:12.960
One, then, can configure
and manage Istio

00:39:12.960 --> 00:39:16.440
through its control
plane functionality.

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:18.960
So, it allows
Zotkix in this case

00:39:18.960 --> 00:39:24.310
to distribute policy
throughout the mesh

00:39:24.310 --> 00:39:30.600
as well as telemetry
configuration, audit policy,

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:34.550
network accessibility
rules, and so on.

00:39:34.550 --> 00:39:38.100
And configuration is consumed
by all these envoy proxies--

00:39:38.100 --> 00:39:43.800
both an ingress proxy if present
as well as the sidecar proxies.

00:39:43.800 --> 00:39:48.740
And it's the policies enforced
by the infrastructure.

00:39:48.740 --> 00:39:54.010
So, in particular, why should
you use Istio security?

00:39:54.010 --> 00:39:57.830
Because it delivers
authentication, authorization,

00:39:57.830 --> 00:39:59.990
and all that as part
of the platform.

00:39:59.990 --> 00:40:02.710
So, you don't need to burden
your application developers

00:40:02.710 --> 00:40:04.180
with getting that right.

00:40:04.180 --> 00:40:07.540
And, in addition, you get
consistent application

00:40:07.540 --> 00:40:09.110
of those policies.

00:40:09.110 --> 00:40:13.030
So, much easier to
complete compliance

00:40:13.030 --> 00:40:15.293
activities within
your environment.

00:40:19.000 --> 00:40:21.130
OK, so, let's give
an example scenario

00:40:21.130 --> 00:40:24.070
where Zotkix is trying
to use this tool

00:40:24.070 --> 00:40:30.150
to provide access management to
their on-premises applications.

00:40:30.150 --> 00:40:34.110
In this example, it's deployed
in the GKE on-prem cluster--

00:40:34.110 --> 00:40:35.970
in particular
Kubernetes cluster--

00:40:35.970 --> 00:40:39.200
but Istio can also
be used to protect

00:40:39.200 --> 00:40:41.730
our workloads and more
traditional on-premise data

00:40:41.730 --> 00:40:46.460
centers that are not
based on Kubernetes.

00:40:46.460 --> 00:40:51.880
So here, when the corp user is
authenticated using credentials

00:40:51.880 --> 00:40:55.320
on the corp environment
and they make a request--

00:40:55.320 --> 00:40:58.220
let's say to the
Zotkix HR application--

00:40:58.220 --> 00:41:02.720
the ingress proxy will
terminate the TLS connection

00:41:02.720 --> 00:41:08.160
and enforce that their request
includes user credentials.

00:41:08.160 --> 00:41:11.310
Later, as the request
travels down the stack,

00:41:11.310 --> 00:41:16.170
you can have policy being
applied at every hub.

00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:20.640
So, for instance,
if you are accessing

00:41:20.640 --> 00:41:24.990
a method that requires
administrative privileges,

00:41:24.990 --> 00:41:28.680
the sidecar envoy
can require that you

00:41:28.680 --> 00:41:32.310
have those privileges
because you belong

00:41:32.310 --> 00:41:35.280
to a particular privileged
group that has the ability

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:36.990
to perform that operation.

00:41:36.990 --> 00:41:41.900
And, in between
hops, you still can

00:41:41.900 --> 00:41:44.510
leverage both the
initial requesting user

00:41:44.510 --> 00:41:46.655
identity as well as
the pure identity

00:41:46.655 --> 00:41:50.730
to perform authorization.

00:41:50.730 --> 00:41:55.820
Here, we see a scenario where
the employee is accessing

00:41:55.820 --> 00:41:58.300
from an overseas location.

00:41:58.300 --> 00:42:03.610
So, in this scenario,
Zotkix decided

00:42:03.610 --> 00:42:05.645
to use the Identity
Aware Proxy--

00:42:05.645 --> 00:42:09.940
IAP-- to take advantage
of Google's fast network--

00:42:09.940 --> 00:42:11.450
globe-spanning network.

00:42:11.450 --> 00:42:14.260
So, many of our
customers actually

00:42:14.260 --> 00:42:19.780
use IP to route both traffic
to their cloud applications

00:42:19.780 --> 00:42:22.060
as well as on-premises
applications,

00:42:22.060 --> 00:42:26.980
because of the advantage of the
Google network connectivity.

00:42:26.980 --> 00:42:29.350
And, by using IAP
in addition, you

00:42:29.350 --> 00:42:32.950
get access to the context
aware authorization rules

00:42:32.950 --> 00:42:34.450
that IAP provides.

00:42:34.450 --> 00:42:37.750
For instance, you can configure
that some app operations

00:42:37.750 --> 00:42:40.240
require second factor
authentication,

00:42:40.240 --> 00:42:45.370
or that the device
is in a good state.

00:42:45.370 --> 00:42:48.970
Now, let's look at how's Zotkix
can use the same architecture

00:42:48.970 --> 00:42:51.710
to protect their cloud apps.

00:42:51.710 --> 00:42:55.380
They have extended the
identity system to GCP

00:42:55.380 --> 00:42:58.810
by using cloud identity.

00:42:58.810 --> 00:43:01.360
By leveraging the
Identity Aware Proxy,

00:43:01.360 --> 00:43:05.110
they have authentication,
authorization

00:43:05.110 --> 00:43:07.720
at ingress, which
simplifies the management

00:43:07.720 --> 00:43:09.060
for the applications.

00:43:09.060 --> 00:43:11.050
And, in this scenario,
we showed that this

00:43:11.050 --> 00:43:12.820
can be used to
protect the legacy

00:43:12.820 --> 00:43:17.010
application, such as a
database VM running in GCP.

00:43:21.660 --> 00:43:25.710
Employees also can have access
to the GKE apps controlled

00:43:25.710 --> 00:43:28.270
this way.

00:43:28.270 --> 00:43:35.020
Again, similar, consistent view
between the on-premise setting

00:43:35.020 --> 00:43:40.165
and the cloud setting by
leveraging Istio and IAP.

00:43:44.800 --> 00:43:50.560
In addition to protecting
employee-based authentication,

00:43:50.560 --> 00:43:53.220
you can use this
infrastructure to protect

00:43:53.220 --> 00:43:55.990
your consumer-oriented
applications--

00:43:55.990 --> 00:43:57.860
the same architecture.

00:43:57.860 --> 00:44:01.640
And one of the advantages
here is that authentication,

00:44:01.640 --> 00:44:03.490
authorization, and audit--

00:44:03.490 --> 00:44:06.400
they permeate the entire
service stack, which

00:44:06.400 --> 00:44:09.620
reduces trust between systems.

00:44:09.620 --> 00:44:12.550
You don't need to trust the
assertion of the user identity

00:44:12.550 --> 00:44:14.890
from the front end
in the back end.

00:44:14.890 --> 00:44:17.740
You can remove this
amount of service

00:44:17.740 --> 00:44:22.180
to service trust, which
facilitates compliance

00:44:22.180 --> 00:44:25.450
against requirements--
audit requirements--

00:44:25.450 --> 00:44:28.030
and it also just
reduces insider risk.

00:44:31.730 --> 00:44:33.840
Another feature of
this architecture

00:44:33.840 --> 00:44:37.050
is that it has a strong
sense of pure identity.

00:44:37.050 --> 00:44:40.830
By using neutral
TLS, you can have

00:44:40.830 --> 00:44:43.980
strong assurances about the
peer that is connecting.

00:44:43.980 --> 00:44:47.280
So here, in the
cases at back end

00:44:47.280 --> 00:44:50.010
have a very strong confidence
that the request indeed

00:44:50.010 --> 00:44:51.630
comes from its front end.

00:44:54.410 --> 00:44:58.610
So, finally, we go back
to what we talked about

00:44:58.610 --> 00:45:01.640
earlier with workload identity.

00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:05.930
You can enable workload
identity in GKE

00:45:05.930 --> 00:45:12.860
and now have fully managed
credentials for your workloads

00:45:12.860 --> 00:45:17.280
where you can directly grant
access to IAM, to your workload

00:45:17.280 --> 00:45:19.390
identity.

00:45:19.390 --> 00:45:22.990
And you also make it much
easier and secure to consume

00:45:22.990 --> 00:45:27.370
any GCP services, such as
cloud store or Stackdriver

00:45:27.370 --> 00:45:28.150
from metrics.

00:45:28.150 --> 00:45:29.710
In this scenario,
stack driver is

00:45:29.710 --> 00:45:34.720
providing the matrix telemetry
infrastructure for Istio.

00:45:37.650 --> 00:45:39.050
So, tying it
together, we can see

00:45:39.050 --> 00:45:41.330
that in a hybrid
environment, Zotkix

00:45:41.330 --> 00:45:44.990
can leverage both
open-source historic texture

00:45:44.990 --> 00:45:48.800
and Google managed services,
such as IP and Stackdriver,

00:45:48.800 --> 00:45:51.320
in a well-integrated
environment that

00:45:51.320 --> 00:45:55.010
delivers consistent,
transparent, and auditable

00:45:55.010 --> 00:45:57.540
policy enforcement.

00:45:57.540 --> 00:45:59.640
And, in addition to the
integration of workload

00:45:59.640 --> 00:46:05.010
identity with IAM, Zotkix can
seamlessly consume GCP services

00:46:05.010 --> 00:46:08.190
from their GKE apps
with improved security

00:46:08.190 --> 00:46:12.080
and eliminating the need
to manage credentials.

00:46:12.080 --> 00:46:16.740
So, let's recap.

00:46:16.740 --> 00:46:19.770
Summing up, we have seen
that CSP and GCP provide

00:46:19.770 --> 00:46:23.700
robust capabilities to help you
easily connect to your existing

00:46:23.700 --> 00:46:25.500
identify solutions.

00:46:25.500 --> 00:46:28.830
These capabilities enable
you to uniformly manage

00:46:28.830 --> 00:46:32.070
hybrid applications
with GKE and Istio,

00:46:32.070 --> 00:46:33.750
and they allow you
to easily secure

